<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Cleartext HTTP is permitted so the reader can point translation at a model
    running on their own machine, or sync with a Lukia server on their own
    network. Such a server has no certificate, and telling somebody to obtain
    one before they can use it is not a real answer.

    The base rule is broader than the app actually allows. Android's
    configuration matches on domains, and it cannot say "private address ranges
    only" - a home network's addresses are not knowable in advance - so the real
    restriction lives in code, in `isAllowedEndpoint`: plain HTTP is
    refused to anything that is not loopback or a private range, before a
    request is made.

    What the configuration can do, and does below, is take the hosted services
    out of the base rule's reach. Those are the addresses that carry the
    reader's API key and whole passages of their book, they are the ones an
    attacker on the network would want to see downgraded, and none of them has
    any business being reached over plain HTTP. Naming them here means the
    platform refuses it whatever the app asks for.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />

    <domain-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">api.anthropic.com</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">api.openai.com</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">googleapis.com</domain>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">wiktionary.org</domain>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
